3755	3774	3585	3585	Harriet	30	" Well , my dears , how does your book go on?-- Have you got any thing fresh ? "
3775	3825	3826	3826	She	173	" Yes , papa ; we have something to read you , something quite fresh . A piece of paper was found on the table this morning-- ( dropt , we suppose , by a fairy)-- containing a very pretty charade , and we have just copied it in . "
3882	3939	3871	3871	she	173	" Aye , that 's very just , indeed , that 's very properly said . Very true . ' Woman , lovely woman . ' It is such a pretty charade , my dear , that I can easily guess what fairy brought it.-- Nobody could have written so prettily , but you , Emma . "
3960	4095	3957	3957	he	35	" Ah ! it is no difficulty to see who you take after ! Your dear mother was so clever at all those things ! If I had but her memory ! But I can remember nothing;-- not even that particular riddle which you have heard me mention ; I can only recollect the first stanza ; and there are several . Kitty , a fair but frozen maid , Kindled a flame I yet deplore , The hood - wink'd boy I called to aid , Though of his near approach afraid , So fatal to my suit before . And that is all that I can recollect of it-- but it is very clever all the way through . But I think , my dear , you said you had got it . "
